<article class="max-w-2xl mx-auto flex flex-col gap-10 leading-relaxed">
  <header class="flex flex-col gap-3">
    <p class="eyebrow">Guide</p>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl leading-tight">How this course works</h1>
    <p class="text-muted">
      What each part of the course is for, where the Irish you hear comes from, and why it's built the way it is.
      This is a closed beta, so things change often, and what you notice is the most useful thing you can send back.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="text-sm flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
      <a href="#first" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">First, the best way</a>
      <a href="#approach" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">The approach</a>
      <a href="#today" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Today</a>
      <a href="#lessons" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Lessons and words</a>
      <a href="#voices" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Whose voice you hear</a>
      <a href="#speaking" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Speaking</a>
      <a href="#listening" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Listening</a>
      <a href="#native-speakers" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Native speakers</a>
      <a href="#your-recordings" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">Your recordings</a>
      <% if current_user&.admin? %>
        <a href="#editors" class="underline underline-offset-2 text-muted hover:text-ink">For editors</a>
      <% end %>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <section id="first" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4 card px-4 py-5">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">First, the best way to learn</h2>
    <p>
      The best way to learn Irish is to spend time in the Gaeltacht, among people who speak it.
      If you can't, the next best is to find a native speaker, become friends, and learn from them.
    </p>
    <p>
      This course doesn't replace either. It's for people who can't get to native speakers easily, and for going alongside time with them.
      That goes double for the spoken conversation here, which is simulated: it's practice for talking to people, not a substitute.
    </p>
    <p>
      The course is free, and always will be. We're Irish speakers ourselves, and the only goal is more speakers in North Connacht.
      The one possible exception is asking native speakers directly: if that's ever paid, all of the money will go to the speaker.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="approach" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">The approach</h2>
    <p>
      The course follows <em>Learning Irish</em> by Mícheál Ó Siadhail, lesson by lesson, adapted for the Irish of Mayo.
      Most courses teach Connemara or the standard; this one aims for what is still spoken in Erris, Achill, Tourmakeady and the rest of the county.
    </p>
    <p class="card px-4 py-4">
      A hundred years ago far more of Mayo spoke Irish. In 1925 the new state counted Irish speakers district by district along the west coast,
      and in 1926 it drew the first official map of the Gaeltacht. Abairt has that map:
      <a href="<%= OnboardingsHelper::GAELTACHT_1926_URL %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="underline underline-offset-2">find your own town or parish and see how it was classed in 1926</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Grammar and word lists matter, but a language is learnt mostly by hearing it and using it.
      So the course mixes four kinds of practice, each with its own colour on the Today screen:
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
      <li><strong>Learn</strong>: the lesson itself, and reviewing its words.</li>
      <li><strong>Practise</strong>: a text chat with an AI tutor about the lesson.</li>
      <li><strong>Speak</strong>: a short spoken conversation about the lesson.</li>
      <li><strong>Listen</strong>: real Mayo speakers, from single words up to whole conversations.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Two rules run through all of it. Wherever possible you hear real native speakers from Mayo rather than a computer.
      And the Irish in the course isn't written by AI: the one place an AI makes up Irish is the spoken conversation, where every reply is labelled as such.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="today" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Today</h2>
    <p>
      The Today screen suggests what to do next, built from where you are: words due for review, the lesson you're on,
      a conversation about it, something to listen to, and any answers from native speakers.
      Work down the list as far as you have time for. Nothing is compulsory, and the list is different tomorrow.
    </p>
    <p>
      Under <strong>So far</strong> you'll see the words you know, the minutes you've spent speaking, and the minutes you've spent listening to whole conversations.
      Minutes spent using the language are a better measure of progress than lessons finished.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="lessons" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Lessons and words</h2>
    <p>
      Each lesson has its words and phrases, grammar notes from the book, and sometimes an introduction video.
      <strong>Any Irish on a lesson page can be heard</strong>: tap the speaker button beside it, or select any piece of text and tap <strong>Hear it</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The quiz asks each word both ways, Irish to English and English to Irish. A word counts as known once you've got it right both ways.
      Words you get right come back after longer and longer gaps (a day, three days, a week, three weeks), and a word you get wrong starts again.
      This is spaced repetition: you spend your time on the words you're about to forget, not the ones you already know.
    </p>
    <p>The Lessons page has three groups:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
      <li><strong>Learning Irish</strong>: the course itself, in order. Today follows these.</li>
      <li><strong>Mayo Irish vocabulary</strong>: word lists by theme, most with recordings of Mayo speakers.</li>
      <li><strong>DIAS field recordings</strong>: notes on the Irish of Foxford and Erris from Heinrich Wagner's survey, for reference.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="voices" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Whose voice you hear</h2>
    <p>There are two kinds of audio in the course, and it always says which one you're hearing.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Real speakers.</strong> The themed word lists come with their own recordings.
      Beyond those, recordings come from Abairt, a growing collection of Mayo Irish recorded from native speakers and transcribed line by line.
      A speech recogniser checks that an Abairt recording really says the word, an editor approves it before you hear it, and it's credited to its speaker.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>A synthetic voice.</strong> Where there's no recording, the course uses ABAIR, the Irish speech technology from Trinity College Dublin,
      with its voice Bríd, the only Mayo voice it has. It's good for getting close to the sound of a word or sentence, but it is a computer:
      the rhythm can be flat, and it doesn't replace hearing people.
    </p>
    <p>
      That's why recordings of real people always come first, and why more of the course will use them as the collection grows.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="speaking" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Speaking</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Conversation</strong> is a short spoken chat about the lesson. Tap the microphone, say something, and tap again to send; you can type instead.
      Your speech goes through ABAIR's recogniser, the reply is written by AI and read out in the Bríd voice, and <strong>Show English</strong> helps when you're stuck.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the only place in the course where an AI writes Irish, which is why each reply is labelled <strong>AI-generated</strong>.
      The replies come from ABAIR's own conversational AI where possible, and from OpenAI when that isn't available.
      They can contain mistakes. If a reply looks wrong, tap <strong>Flag</strong> and an editor will check it. Nothing from a conversation ever becomes course material.
    </p>
    <p>
      The point is to say things out loud, often, without the pressure of a real person waiting.
      Every recorded turn adds to your minutes spoken; typed turns don't count.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Practise</strong> is a text chat with an AI tutor (OpenAI) that quizzes you on the lesson's grammar and words, one question at a time, and explains as it goes.
      It quotes Irish only from the lesson itself and doesn't make up its own; if you need Irish the lesson doesn't have, it says so.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="listening" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Listening</h2>
    <p>Listening builds up in three steps, from words to phrases to whole conversations.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Words.</strong> When a lesson's words have recordings of Mayo speakers, Today offers them, or a theme from the vocabulary lists when they don't.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Phrases.</strong> Once you've finished a lesson, Today offers one short phrase a day in which a Mayo speaker uses a word from it, the shortest first.
      You're told which word to listen for. Play it as often as you like, slow it down, and only then open <strong>Show the words</strong>.
      Then say whether you made it out. Phrases are cut from real conversations, so they're quick, run together, and sometimes rough at the edges, which is how people really talk.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Whole conversations.</strong> Each phrase leads on to the conversation it came from, and about once a week Today suggests one.
      The words follow along as it plays, and you can tap a line to hear it again or turn on the English.
      Don't try to catch every word. Listen for the rhythm of the talk and the words you know; understanding builds up from there.
    </p>
    <p>
      Everything you've listened to is kept under <strong>Your listening</strong>, linked from Today, so you can go back to the phrases you didn't get.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="native-speakers" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Native speakers</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Ask a native speaker</strong>, on any lesson, lets you record a question, about a word, a sound, or how something is said in Mayo, and send it to native speakers.
      It goes to them through Abairt on Telegram, and one of them answers by voice when they're free.
      The answer shows on Today and on the questions page.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are real people giving their time, so you listen back before sending, and short, specific questions work best.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="your-recordings" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4">
    <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Your recordings</h2>
    <p>
      When you speak in a conversation, the recording is sent to ABAIR to be turned into text, and the conversation's text goes to ABAIR's AI, or to OpenAI when that isn't available, for a reply.
      The tutor chat goes to OpenAI.
      A question for a native speaker is kept on Abairt, where the speakers who answer can hear it, labelled with the first part of your email address.
      Nothing you record is used as course material.
    </p>
  </section>

  <% if current_user&.admin? %>
    <%= render "editors" %>
  <% end %>

  <% if authenticated? %>
    <p class="text-sm text-muted border-t border-line pt-6">
      Something unclear, wrong, or missing? Say so. In a beta, that's the most useful thing you can do.
    </p>
  <% else %>
    <section id="joining" class="flex flex-col gap-3 scroll-mt-4 border-t border-line pt-6">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl">Joining</h2>
      <p>
        The course is invite only until it's ready.
        <%= link_to "Register", new_registration_path, class: "underline underline-offset-2" %> and your account will be checked before you get in,
        or <%= link_to "sign in", new_session_path, class: "underline underline-offset-2" %> if you already have one.
      </p>
    </section>
  <% end %>
</article>
